# The document-to-document references a filing actually makes. One row per (filing, foreign
# notation) pair it reaches through a part or through the layers an elimination is stated between.
#
# This is what an `import` is
#   And `pm.filing_identity` is not it. That table is every document declaring its own uri:
#   `asserted_by` reads *the filing, about itself*. In BPMN that is `targetNamespace`, one per
#   document. An `import` is a document naming another one, and nothing in the model files those
#   directly: they are derived from the references that cross a document boundary.
#
# Parts are not the only reach
#   The parts are not the only place this model reaches out of a filing. There are two
#   `pm:ForeignId` references: an `asrt:Part`, and an `asrt:Elimination/between`. Both name a
#   notation and a layer in another document. With no relation calling the second one a
#   reference, every enumeration of what this model reaches returns the parts alone. In this
#   corpus the union changes nothing, because every document a `between` names is already reached
#   by a part. That is luck and not construction, and a count cannot tell the difference, which is
#   why the union is taken here rather than argued about.
#
# A count settles identity against import
#   An identity table is a `targetNamespace` and not an `import`. Declared as
#   `|import| = |notation|`, the law expects one import per notation and the emitter writes fewer,
#   one per document reached, and fewer is right: `asserted_by` reads the filing about itself,
#   where an import is a document naming another. A law is worth having exactly when it can
#   contradict the person who wrote it.
-- diagrams/calls.sqlc's foreign parts, and the filed layers an elimination is stated between.
SELECT DISTINCT x.composition, x.part_notation
FROM (
    SELECT c.composition, c.part_notation
    FROM ( :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc) ) c
    WHERE NOT c.is_local
    UNION
    SELECT b.composition, b.notation
    FROM ( :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc) ) b
) x
